The current bug

The current model makes a rival’s longitudinal race distance depend on its screen-space position relative to Player 1:

metersOf(R) effectively does:

G.meters + (playerY - R.y) * 0.075

Meanwhile normal rival motion in updateRival() does approximately:

R.y += (G.speed - R.abs) * dt

and then:

R.y = clamp(R.y, -30000, H + 30000)

This means R.y is doing two jobs at once:

1. presentation/camera-relative screen position;
2. authoritative race progress.

Those concepts must not be the same thing.

The failure becomes obvious for a racer far ahead.

Suppose a rival reaches the existing upper-road clamp:

R.y === -30000

The rival may still have:

R.abs === BASE_SPEED or substantially more.

As long as Player 1 is moving, G.meters increases, so metersOf(R) can continue increasing even though the rival itself is stuck at R.y === -30000.

But when Player 1 is destroyed, update() deliberately drives:

G.speed -> 0

which is correct behavior for the wrecked player.

Now G.meters stops advancing.

The distant rival continues trying to execute:

R.y += (0 - R.abs) * dt

but that would put it below -30000, so the clamp immediately forces it back to -30000.

Therefore:

* R.y no longer changes;
* G.meters no longer changes;
* metersOf(R) no longer changes.

The rival is still notionally driving according to R.abs, but its authoritative race distance is frozen.

The 30000px limit represents only roughly 2250 metres at the game’s 0.075 metres/pixel conversion, so a sufficiently separated field can genuinely reach this condition during a long race.

This is a camera-coordinate leak into simulation state.

Required simulation model

Give every rival an authoritative longitudinal world/race distance that exists independently from Player 1’s camera position.

Player 1 may continue to use G.meters as its canonical longitudinal distance.

Every rival should have an equivalent canonical value, for example:

R.m

or another clearly named field.

The exact name is up to you, but there must be one authoritative longitudinal value per racer.

Conceptually:

Player 1:
canonical distance = G.meters

Rival:
canonical distance = R.m

Camera-space rival position should become a projection of those values:

R.y = playerY - (R.m - G.meters) / 0.075

A rival’s race progress must no longer be reconstructed from a bounded screen coordinate.

Rival initialization

When rivals are spawned, initialize their canonical distance so their current starting-grid layout remains exactly the same.

Do not visually change the starting grid.

For example, derive initial R.m from the existing initial R.y/grid offset once during construction, or construct both from the same starting-distance value.

The first frame of a race should be visually and mechanically indistinguishable from the current game.

Normal rival movement

When a live racer is driving, advance its canonical race distance directly from its own physical speed.

At a conceptual level:

R.m += R.abs * dt * 0.075

The player continues advancing with:

G.meters += G.speed * dt * 0.075

Then derive the rival’s camera-relative position from the difference between R.m and G.meters.

Do not advance rival race distance using (G.speed - R.abs).

That expression is useful for calculating relative movement on Player 1’s screen, but it is not the rival’s actual forward race progress.

This distinction is critical.

If Player 1 is stationary and a bot is moving at 420 px/s, the bot should still cover approximately:

420 * 1 * 0.075 = 31.5 metres

during one simulated second.

If Player 1 is wrecked for the normal three-second DEAD_TIME, the rest of the field should continue advancing for those three seconds according to each car’s own speed, boosts, ultimates, slows, shunts, etc.

Do not let a coordinate clamp control race progress

The existing:

clamp(R.y, -30000, H + 30000)

must no longer be capable of freezing or modifying canonical race progress.

Prefer removing the simulation-level clamp entirely and deriving the real camera-relative y from canonical distance.

The finite race distances used by Seren are nowhere near JavaScript numeric limits.

If some rendering safeguard is still genuinely needed, clamp only a temporary value used for drawing/culling. Never write a presentation clamp back into authoritative race state.

Also consider the secondary problem with keeping simulation objects clamped: two racers that are actually hundreds of metres apart could eventually collapse onto the same -30000 coordinate and appear physically colocated to collision or AI code. The fix should ensure a rendering safeguard cannot create false simulation proximity.

Wrecked rivals

Preserve existing wreck behavior.

When a rival itself is destroyed:

* its own forward race distance should stop advancing for the duration of its wreck;
* Player 1 and every other racer continue normally;
* if Player 1’s camera moves during that time, the wrecked rival’s camera-relative y should still update because its fixed world position is being viewed from a moving camera;
* after its wreck timer expires, it resumes from the exact canonical distance at which it was destroyed.

Do not accidentally make a wrecked rival continue racing invisibly.

The required change is independence between racers, not removal of wreck penalties.

Player destruction

destroyCar() and Player 1’s existing wreck lifecycle should retain their intended effects.

It is correct that Player 1’s own speed falls to zero.

It is correct that Player 1 does not accumulate race distance while stopped.

It is incorrect for this to stop another racer’s distance.

Do not solve the issue by advancing G.meters while Player 1 is destroyed. G.meters belongs to Player 1 and should continue meaning Player 1’s actual distance.

Finish-line behavior

A rival must be able to cross the finish line while Player 1 is wrecked and stationary.

checkFinish() already runs during the race. After this fix it must receive a continuously advancing rival distance from metersOf(R).

Verify this explicitly.

Example scenario:

* Player 1 is destroyed.
* G.speed === 0.
* a bot is still alive and approaching G.finishAt.
* the bot continues accumulating distance.
* it crosses G.finishAt.
* R.finished receives the correct placing immediately.
* the result is added to G.results.
* Player 1 may still be in the middle of the wreck timer.

Do not defer AI finishes until Player 1 respawns.

Finish rollout / parking

Preserve the current finish parking system.

R.parkM is already expressed as an absolute road-distance concept and should remain consistent with the new canonical racer distance.

Decide carefully whether a finished racer’s canonical distance should follow its rollout or whether parkM remains the separate post-finish presentation distance. Either is acceptable if the current visible behavior and race-order semantics are preserved.

Do not break:

* finishing order;
* the staggered parking staircase;
* parkLaneFor();
* parkMeters();
* the current easing;
* already-finished cars remaining correctly placed while other cars continue racing.

metersOf()

After the migration, metersOf(R) should return the rival’s canonical longitudinal distance rather than reverse-engineering it from R.y.

Keep metersOf() as the central accessor if that reduces churn. A large amount of the game already correctly asks metersOf() instead of reading longitudinal state directly.

That means AI, HUD standings, targeting, race order, finish detection, and mechanics can benefit from the architectural fix without each subsystem inventing its own workaround.

Avoid scattering direct R.m reads everywhere unless there is a strong reason.

Audit every direct rival y mutation

This part is important.

Search the entire repository for code that directly modifies a racer’s y.

Classify each occurrence as either:

Camera projection only
The racer’s world distance did not change. Only its location relative to Player 1’s camera changed.

or:

Actual longitudinal movement / teleport
The racer’s real position on the track changed and therefore its canonical distance must change.

Pay special attention to:

* updateRival()
* wreck handling
* finish rollout
* rebaseWorld()
* teleportRacerToPose()
* contact/rear-end displacement
* any code that assigns who.y
* Neela’s position exchange

Do not leave hidden code paths that mutate R.y without keeping canonical distance coherent.

A useful invariant is:

For every active rival, when using an unclamped camera projection,

R.y ≈ playerY - (metersOf(R) - G.meters) / 0.075

Any real longitudinal position change should update canonical metres first and then project to y.

Neela and world rebasing

Be particularly careful with Neela.

Current architecture deliberately supports racerWorldPose(), teleportRacerToPose(), and rebaseWorld() so Neela can exchange actual world positions even though Player 1 is the camera anchor.

Keep that behavior.

racerWorldPose(R) should naturally return the new canonical rival distance.

Teleporting a rival to a pose must set its canonical longitudinal distance as well as its lane/x and camera projection.

When Player 1 is teleported through rebaseWorld():

* G.meters changes;
* scenery/hazards/world-space presentation shifts appropriately;
* unrelated rivals’ canonical distances must NOT change;
* their camera-relative y values must change only because the camera moved.

Do not double-apply the rebase to both R.m and R.y.

The existing documented invariant that unrelated racers stay at the same world location through a Player 1 rebase must remain true.

AI behavior

Bots must continue thinking and driving while off-screen and while Player 1 is wrecked.

Difficulty must continue affecting decision quality only. Do not add rubber-banding or a special wreck bonus.

Existing values such as:

* R.abs
* boost state
* ultimate state
* slow state
* shunt state
* AI think timers

should continue working normally.

Distance-based AI decisions should use canonical race distance through metersOf().

Do not make bots chase Player 1 simply because the camera belongs to Player 1.

They are racing the whole field.

Local multiplayer

This fix must also work in Local mode.

Player 1 being destroyed must not suspend P2, P3, P4, or any bot.

Likewise, if a human-controlled rival is far ahead of Player 1, that racer must continue making independent race progress even if Player 1’s car is stationary.

The canonical-distance solution should apply equally to bot rivals and human-controlled rivals because they share updateRival().

Do not create separate physics for bots and local humans.

What NOT to do

Do not fix this by merely increasing 30000 to a larger number.

Do not remove the clamp and leave R.y as authoritative race state.

Do not make G.meters advance while the player is wrecked.

Do not add a special if (G.dead) distance increment.

Do not calculate standings differently only while the player is destroyed.

Do not teleport distant racers forward after the player respawns.

Do not implement rubber-banding.

Do not pause AI until racers return to the visible screen.

Do not create a second simplified simulation for off-screen racers.

There should be one race simulation. Visibility is presentation only.

Regression tests to add

Add deterministic regression coverage for this bug, preferably to tools/progression-check.mjs or another existing simulation-focused suite.

At minimum cover all of these cases:

1. Put a live rival at or beyond the old -30000 far-ahead condition.
2. Give it a known R.abs, such as BASE_SPEED.
3. Destroy Player 1 or otherwise put Player 1 into the real wreck state with zero movement.
4. Run one simulated second.
5. Assert that the rival gained approximately 31.5 metres at tier 0:
    BASE_SPEED * 1s * 0.075.
6. Assert that Player 1 gained essentially zero metres during the stationary wreck interval.
7. Put a rival shortly before G.finishAt, destroy Player 1, simulate enough time for the rival to cross, and assert that the rival receives a finishing place while G.dead > 0.
8. Verify monotonic rival progress through the entire Player 1 wreck/respawn cycle: no freeze, backwards jump, teleport, or catch-up correction.
9. Verify a rival that is itself wrecked does NOT gain distance during its own wreck.
10. Verify rebaseWorld() / Neela position exchange keeps unrelated racers’ canonical world distances unchanged.
11. Verify standings obtained through placeOf() remain correct for very large separations.
12. Verify Local mode human rivals continue progressing while Player 1 is wrecked.

Where practical, construct the regression so the old implementation fails for the precise reason described above rather than merely checking a new field exists.

Documentation

Update docs/ARCHITECTURE.md.

The current Where a racer actually is section says that rival position is fundamentally a screen offset converted by metersOf().

That will no longer be accurate.

Document the new invariant clearly:

* every racer has canonical longitudinal race distance;
* Player 1’s canonical value is G.meters;
* rivals carry their own canonical value;
* R.y is a camera-space projection of the difference;
* simulation progress exists independently of visibility and independently of Player 1’s movement;
* player/world rebasing changes the camera/world presentation without changing unrelated racers’ absolute track positions.

Update docs/TUNING.md if its explanation of metre/pixel conversion becomes inaccurate.

